#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069920 :tutorial/content "Consider the plane region enclosed by the curve\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nthe curve\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\nand the vertical line $x=2$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/65/651d5d0d9ccafdeb94608e74086136b121c394670afbea669c3e99606f9756aa.png)\n\nWe rotate this region $360^\\circ$ about the $x$-axis to get a solid of revolution, as shown below. How can we find the volume of this solid?\n\n![](images/7a/7a1e4b8d0a27283cad7a0b0e0f2b23f1a45d0e560582a61f84c0f90a4f61e039.png)\n\nTo find the volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by the outer curve $y=f(x)$, the inner curve $y=g(x)$, and the vertical lines $x=a$ and $x=b$ is rotated $360^\\circ$ about the $x$-axis, we use the following formula:\n\n$$\nV = \\pi \\int_{a}^{b} \\Big(\\big[ \\underbrace{f(x)}_\\text{outer} \\big]^2 - \\big[ \\underbrace{g(x)}_\\text{inner} \\big]^2 \\Big) \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThis method of computing the volume is called the **washer method** because it involves breaking up the region into many thin washers (one such washer is shown below).\n\n![](images/29/2920f308f15ef81eaff0ba431d0f4eec3ccd9826fbde8f126242b0493f1f8e32.png)\n\nThe cross-sectional area of each washer corresponds to the region between two discs of radius $f(x)$ and $g(x)$, so the area of each washer cross-section is\n\n$$\n\\pi [f(x)]^2 - \\pi [g(x)]^2\n$$\n\nand this is what we integrate.\n\nIn our case, the outer curve is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nand the inner curve is\n\n$$\ng(x) = \\dfrac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\nso the volume is given by\n\n$$\nV = π\\int_{0}^{2}(\\sqrt{x})^{2} - (\\frac{1}{2}x)^{2}dx\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\nV = π\\int_{0}^{2}(x - \\frac{x^{2}}{4})dx\n$$\n\nEvaluating this integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= π(\\int_{0}^{2}xdx - \\frac{1}{4}\\int_{0}^{2}x^{2}dx) \\\\\n&= \\left.π(\\frac{x^{2}}{2}\\right|_{0}^{2} - \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\left.\\frac{x^{3}}{3}\\right|_{0}^{2}) \\\\\n&= π((\\frac{4}{2} - 0) - \\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{8}{3} - 0)) \\\\\n&= π(2 - \\frac{2}{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the volume of the solid is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\ncubic units." :tutorial/id #uuid "26f7c4eb-b5c8-505b-9621-32b5c0241376" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1058 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069921 :tutorial/content "Consider the plane region bounded by the curve\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{y}\n$$\n\nthe curve $x=y^2$, and the horizontal line\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/90/908d37e7b02ffb2b585c29bd11e3adfc92b005e0d7b205bcea258461b98d9ee6.png)\n\nWe rotate this region $360^\\circ$ about the $y$-axis to get a solid of revolution, as shown below. How can we find the volume of this solid?\n\n![](images/b6/b675045622be931c60199474e9fe5131ad736f09061746f1d1f966690bd6d5cd.png)\n\nTo find the volume of this solid, we can use the same washer method approach that we used earlier for the $x$-axis. The only difference is that we need to swap $x$ and $y$ in our formula.\n\nSo, to find the volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by the outer curve $x=f(y)$, the inner curve $x=g(y)$, and the horizontal lines $y=c$ and $y=d$ is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis, we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= \\int_{c}^{d}π([f(y)_(⏟)_(outer)]^{2} - [g(y)_(⏟)_(inner)]^{2})dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the same washer method as before, the only difference is that we need to swap $x$ and $y$. It involves breaking up the region into many thin washers (one such washer is shown below).\n\n![](images/ba/ba94d5e2ac72140fadd80298390d1b0f36b00f46037c8356110e79d4ac6d50dd.png)\n\nThe cross-sectional area of each washer corresponds to the region between two discs of radius $f(y)$ and $g(y)$, so the area of each washer cross-section is\n\n$$\n\\pi [f(y)]^2 - \\pi [g(y)]^2\n$$\n\nand this is what we integrate.\n\nIn our example, the outer curve is\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{y}\n$$\n\nand the inner curve is $x=y^2$, so the volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= \\int_{0}^{1/2}π([\\sqrt{y}]^{2} - [y^{2}]^{2})dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= π\\int_{0}^{1/2}(y - y^{4})dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating this integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= π(\\int_{0}^{1/2}ydy - \\int_{0}^{1/2}y^{4}dy) \\\\\n&= \\left.π(\\frac{y^{2}}{2}\\right|_{0}^{1/2} - \\left.\\frac{y^{5}}{5}\\right|_{0}^{1/2}) \\\\\n&= π((\\frac{1}{8} - 0) - (\\frac{1}{160} - 0)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{19π}{160}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1c7872ee-a22d-525d-8be2-58494ec5527b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1061 :tutorial/title "Rotation About the Y-Axis"}]}
